/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A component is one of the smallest set of chemical species needed to make up every phase of the system.
   - A phase is a region uniform in composition and physical structure, as in 5.1.
   - The degrees of freedom F are the number of intensive properties (temperature, pressure and the composition of each phase) that can be changed independently without making a phase appear or disappear.
   - F is the number of degrees of freedom; C is the number of components; P is the number of phases in equilibrium; the 2 stands for the two intensive properties every phase shares, temperature and pressure.
   - The rule counts intensive properties only.
   - Count the unknowns and the equations.
   - Equilibrium between phases needs one more condition for each component: it must have the same chemical potential, the Gibbs function per mole, in every phase.
   - Each equation removes one free choice, so the degrees of freedom are the unknowns left over.
   - For a pure substance C = 1, so F = 3 − P.
   - Superheated steam in a pipe has F = 2, so you may pick 10 bar and 250 °C independently and look up the state.
*/
