/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Heat 1 kg of water at 20 °C and 1 atm.
   - More heat at 1 atm does not raise the temperature.
   - hf is the specific enthalpy of saturated liquid (kJ/kg); hg is the specific enthalpy of saturated vapour (kJ/kg); hfg is the latent heat of vaporisation (kJ/kg).
   - Wet steam is saturated liquid and saturated vapour together.
   - x = mg / (mf + mg)
   - mg is the mass of vapour (kg); mf is the mass of liquid (kg); x is the dryness fraction, no unit. x = 0 is saturated liquid; x = 1 is saturated vapour; 0 < x < 1 is wet steam.
   - Take 1 kg of wet steam.
   - h is the specific enthalpy of wet steam (kJ/kg); x is dryness fraction; hf and hfg are taken at the same pressure.
   - Specific volume v and specific entropy s follow the same linear rule, because each is a mass-weighted property in the wet region.
   - v = vf + x (vg − vf) and s = sf + x (sg − sf)
*/
