/* book Worked example 2: Q: 2 kg of water at 25 °C (h = 104.8 kJ/kg) is pumped into a boiler at 10 bar and turned into steam. At 10 bar, Tsat = 179.9 °C, hf = 763 kJ/kg, hfg = 2015 kJ/kg and hg = 2778 kJ/kg. Find the heat supplied when the steam leaves (a) wet with x = 0.9, (b) dry saturated, (c) superheated to 250 °C, taking cp of superheated steam as 2.1 kJ/(kg·K). Ignore the pump work.
   1. At constant pressure the heat supplied per kg is the rise in enthalpy: q = hfinal − hwater, and Q = m q.
   2. (a) h = hf + x hfg = 763 + 0.9 × 2015 = 763 + 1813.5 = 2576.5 kJ/kg. Q = 2 × (2576.5 − 104.8) = 2 × 2471.7 = 4943.4 kJ.
   3. (b) h = hg = 2778 kJ/kg. Q = 2 × (2778 − 104.8) = 2 × 2673.2 = 5346.4 kJ.
   4. (c) Degree of superheat = 250 − 179.9 = 70.1 K. h = hg + cp (T − Tsat) = 2778 + 2.1 × 70.1 = 2778 + 147.2 = 2925.2 kJ/kg.
   5. Q = 2 × (2925.2 − 104.8) = 2 × 2820.4 = 5640.8 kJ.
   6. Check against the superheated table: at 10 bar and 250 °C it gives h = 2943 kJ/kg. The cp estimate is 18 kJ/kg (0.6 per cent) low, because cp of steam is larger near saturation; use the table when one is given.
   Answer: (a) 4943.4 kJ, (b) 5346.4 kJ, (c) 5640.8 kJ with the cp estimate (5676 kJ with the table value 2943 kJ/kg). */
